> API and interpretation

Candidate lists during a trace are intermediate states, not final maximality
certificates. The direct predecessor search is quadratic in the worst case;
candidate removals themselves are linear in the number of seeds. Widened prefix
scores are floating arithmetic, not exact-real sums; nonfinite inputs,
unrepresentable outputs and nonzero increments lost at prefix precision reject
explicitly. Complete-list histories can also be quadratic.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Ruzzo and Tompa, A Linear Time Algorithm for Finding All Maximal Scoring Subsequences (1999)|https://homes.cs.washington.edu/~ruzzo/papers/maxseq.html]

